 "raw_reply": "{\"item\": \"S446-015\", \"clauses\": {\"estimate_product\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F11\", \"template\": \"Estimate the product: {real_a} × {real_b}\", \"reasoning\": \"F11 asks the learner to estimate the product of two factors drawn from real_a and real_b, which are printed as the factors of the estimated product.\"}, \"2_to_3_digit_factors\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F11\", \"template\": \"Estimate the product: {real_a} × {real_b}\", \"reasoning\": \"real_a prints 2-digit values (10 to 99) and 3-digit values (100 to 984) in its printed_runs, so F11 produces 2- to 3-digit factors.\"}, \"1_to_2_digit_factors\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F11\", \"template\": \"Estimate the product: {real_a} × {real_b}\", \"reasoning\": \"real_b prints 2-digit values only (10 to 98 in printed_runs), so F11 addresses the 2-digit part of this clause. No family prints a 1-digit factor; that shortfall is recorded under Rule 1.\"}, \"estimate_factors\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F06\", \"template\": \"{capitalize} of {a} × {b} = {result}.\", \"reasoning\": \"F06 shows the rounded factors a and b next to the estimated product, which is the estimating of the factors that the clause names.\"}, \"multiples_of_10\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F06\", \"template\": \"{capitalize} of {a} × {b} = {result}.\", \"reasoning\": \"a and b in F06 are printed as multiples of 10 only (10 to 980 and 10 to 100), which is the multiples of 10 the clause names.\"}}, \"failing_families\": [\"F04\", \"F05\", \"F12\"], \"failing_templates\": [\"Use the {b} times table: {b} × {a}.\", \"We need to {mul_phrase} {b} {count_noun}.\", \"{real_a} × {real_b} ≈ {fill_value}\"], \"overall\": \"FAIL\", \"reasoning\": \"F04: the b slot (repeats a generated value, printed 10 to 100 in multiples of 10) is printed as the name of a times table, so a learner is told to use a 40 or 100 times table. Times tables in the declared variants are 2 to 10 only, and the phrase times table is not in cumulative_vocab. This is a concept beyond the lc's gates and beyond grade 3 Q3. F05: count_noun's domain (one of the generator's own choices) includes the singular forms of the phrase, and b is never 1 (printed 10 to 100), so the template prints a plural count with a singular noun after a number greater than 1. That wording is ungrammatical and a grade 3 learner cannot rely on it. F12: fill_value's second domain (a number in a range, the keyed answer offset by 1 to 10, floored at 0, lo 1890, hi 10010) produces the false statements. The statement uses the approximately-equal sign, and an offset of 1 to 10 from a keyed value near 2000 or 10000 is under 1 percent, so the statement is approximately true and its False key can be wrong. The true fill_value members (200, 2000, 2100, 6600) are the keyed answers. Scope shortfall (not a family finding): real_b, the shared domain of F07, F10, F11, F12 and F14, prints only 2-digit values (10 to 98), so 1-digit factors named in the 1_to_2_digit_factors clause are never produced. F03 (hint) prints the split of a multiple of 10 and its arithmetic is consistent with the result; no finding. F06 (hint) and F09 (vocabulary 'missing number' is in cumulative_vocab) show no fault. F07, F08, F09, F10, F11, F13 and F14 are otherwise within vocabulary and grade.\"}",
